  "result": "There is a particular kind of silence that exists just before a city wakes. It is not the absence of sound. Pipes knock inside old walls. A truck sighs at a red light. Somewhere, a baker drops a metal tray, and the noise travels farther than it has any right to.\n\nI like to imagine this hour as the city\u2019s rehearsal. The streets are still deciding what they will become: a shortcut, a meeting place, the setting for an argument someone will remember for years. For now, they are simply streets, holding yesterday\u2019s rain in the shallow curves beside the curb.\n\nA woman opens a caf\u00e9 and sets two chairs outside. This seems like a small act of optimism. She cannot know who will sit there, whether they will be lonely or delighted, whether the coffee will accompany good news. Nevertheless, she arranges the chairs so they face each other.\n\nPerhaps that is most of what civilization amounts to: making arrangements for people we have not met. Planting trees whose shade we will never need. Labeling the fuse box clearly. Leaving a porch light on. The grand speeches matter, but so does the person who puts a spare blanket in the guest room.\n\nSoon the traffic gathers itself. Doors slam, phones ring, and the city begins insisting on its importance. The quiet hour disappears without complaint. Yet those two chairs remain outside the caf\u00e9, waiting patiently for strangers to arrive and turn an ordinary morning into something worth telling someone.",
